---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/site/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/site/Footer.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { REPO_URL } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'Privacy — pigi';
const description = 'What the pigi website and app collect, and what they do not.';
const updated = '2026-11-07';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Header />

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
    <a
      href="/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[13px] text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:text-foreground"
    >
      <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} class="rotate-180" />
      pigi
    </a>

    <h1 class="mt-6 text-[32px] font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.02em] text-foreground">
      Privacy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Last updated {updated}.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 border-t border-border/70 pt-8">
      <div class="markdown-body text-[15px] text-foreground">
        <h2>The app</h2>
        <p>
          pigi has no server of its own. Your sessions, prompts, code and settings stay on your
          machine, in the files pi already uses.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Model requests</strong> go from pi straight to the providers you configure, under
            their terms. pigi does not see, proxy or store them anywhere else.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Update checks</strong> fetch release files from GitHub. GitHub receives that request
            the way it receives any download.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Usage data</h3>
        <p>
          To find crashes and see which features people use, the app sends usage data to pigi's own
          endpoint on Cloudflare:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            that the app started, its version, macOS version and chip, and the default view mode;
          </li>
          <li>that a prompt was sent (never what it said);</li>
          <li>which workspace tabs you switch to and which pigi shortcuts you press;</li>
          <li>
            crash and error reports: the error message and stack trace, with anything that looks
            like a file path, URL, host name, email address or key removed, and which app process
            stopped unexpectedly. Messages are cut short but can still quote a few words from what
            caused the error.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Events are tied to a random id created on your machine, not to you, and never include your
          prompts, replies, code, file contents or project names. Cloudflare looks up the country
          from the connection; the IP address is not stored. Data is kept for three months. The{' '}
          <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/telemetry.md`}>full list of events</a> is in the source.
        </p>
        <p>
          To turn it off, switch off <strong>Settings → Privacy → Share usage data</strong>, which
          also deletes the id, or set <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> in your shell. Builds you make yourself
          send nothing.
        </p>

        <h2>This website</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Analytics:</strong>{' '}
            <a href="https://umami.is">Umami</a> counts page views, referrers, browser, device and country,
            plus clicks on the download and GitHub links. It sets no cookies, and your IP address is used
            only in passing, to tell visitors apart and estimate the country, not kept with the analytics.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Hosting:</strong> the site is served by Cloudflare Pages, which processes requests
            under Cloudflare's own privacy policy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Theme:</strong> a light or dark choice is kept in your browser's local storage and
            never leaves it.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>Open an issue on <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>GitHub</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
